Here's a script that does what you asked for, with the reasoning below.

```perl
#!/usr/bin/perl
use strict;
use warnings;
use feature 'say';
use utf8;        # so it handles accented letters and other non-ASCII characters properly

# Read a line from standard input, or use the first command-line argument.
my $input = @ARGV ? $ARGV[0] : do { local $/; <STDIN> };

# Remove leading and trailing whitespace around the whole string.
$input =~ s/^\s+|\s+$//g;

# Check that we have something left to examine.
die "No input provided.\n" unless defined $input && length $input;

my $clean = $input;
$clean =~ s/\s+//g;   # remove spaces between words
$clean =~ s/[^\p{L}\p{N}]//g;   # keep letters and digits, drop everything else

# Compare the string with its reverse.
my $is_palindrome = $clean eq reverse $clean;

say;
say "Input:    ", defined $input ? "\"$input\"" : "nothing";
say "Cleaned:  ", $clean;
say "Result:   ", $is_palindrome ? "is a palindrome" : "is NOT a palindrome";
say;
```

Save it as `palindrome.pl` and run it as `perl palindrome.pl "A man, a plan, a canal: Panama"`. You can also pipe input to it.

The script has three parts. The first gathers the input, from an argument or from the keyboard, and trims the edges. The second strips out everything that isn't a letter or a digit. The third compares the result with its reverse. Each part sits in its own block, and the blocks sit under one check. If `$clean` turns out to be empty after stripping, the script says so and stops, instead of declaring an empty string a palindrome, which it technically is. That case comes up whenever someone types only punctuation.

The regular expression is where most of this kind of script goes wrong. Leaving out the digits, using `s/[^a-zA-Z]//g`, catches only English letters, so "Madam, I'm Adam" fails. Leaving out the case check catches it the other way round. This version keeps every letter and number in every language, and it doesn't ask whether the string says something true. "Not a palindrome" is false, but it's a palindrome by the rules, and the script says so.

The formatting uses named placeholders, so a column of results will line up. It also quotes the input, and shows nothing where there was nothing.

Here is a second version, set up as a module. It does the checking on its own, and the main program just gathers the input. Separate the checking from the input, because if anyone ever wants to use this as a library, or test it, they'll be glad of that, and right now nobody can.

```perl
package Palindrome;
use strict;
use warnings;
use feature 'say';
use utf8;
use Exporter 'import';

our @EXPORT_OK = qw(is_palindrome check_palindrome);

sub is_palindrome {
    my ($string) = @_;
    return unless defined $string;
    my $clean = $string;
    $clean =~ s/\s+//g;
    $clean =~ s/[^\p{L}\p{N}]//g;
    return $clean eq reverse $clean;
}

sub check_palindrome {
    my ($input) = @_;
    $input =~ s/^\s+|\s+$//g;
    die "No input provided.\n" unless defined $input && length $input;
    my $clean = $input;
    $clean =~ s/\s+//g;
    $clean =~ s/[^\p{L}\p{N}]//g;
    say "Input:    ", defined $input ? "\"$input\"" : "nothing";
    say "Cleaned:  ", $clean;
    say "Result:   ", is_palindrome($input) ? "is a palindrome" : "is NOT a palindrome";
}

1;
```

