To test it, write a file called `palindrome.t`:

```perl
use Test::More;
use Palindrome qw(is_palindrome);

is(is_palindrome("racecar"), 1, "simple word");
is(is_palindrome("A man, a plan, a canal: Panama"), 1, "phrases, mixed case and punctuation");
is(is_palindrome("hello"), 0, "not one");
is(is_palindrome(""), 0, "empty string");
is(is_palindrome("!!!"), 0, "punctuation only");
is(is_palindrome("Madam, I'm Adam"), 1, "punctuation inside the words");

done_testing;
```

Run it with `prove palindrome.t`. The empty string and the string of exclamation marks are both stripped away to nothing, and the script treats them as not palindromes, which is one reasonable reading. Another reading is that an empty string is a palindrome, since nothing in it disagrees. People who work on this disagree about it. If you'd rather count an empty string as one, change `return unless defined $string;` to `return 1 unless length $string;`. I'd keep the check, though. A string of nothing but punctuation that someone typed in to see what happens is most likely a mistake, and the script is better off telling them so.

Two things are left for you to decide. Whether the check should be case-sensitive is a choice about what users expect, and neither choice is wrong. My lean is that it shouldn't be, because nobody checks case when they read a palindrome. If your users read "Racecar" as not a palindrome, that's the first thing they'll write about. The other is how long a string the script should process. Right now it just processes whatever comes in. Very long input with a lot of punctuation gets stripped and then reversed, so the cost is roughly the square of the length, and a few hundred kilobytes of text will take a noticeable time. If it has to handle input of that size, the fast way is to check characters from the two ends moving inward, stopping at the first mismatch. Write that once and test it against this version on some long strings, so that the two agree on everything, and then you'll know which one is right.

Here's the implementation. I've written it as a class with the matching logic separated out, so you can run it from the command line or use the method from other code.

```java
import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.io.InputStreamReader;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.regex.Matcher;
import java.util.regex.Pattern;
import java.util.regex.PatternSyntaxException;

public class LineCounter {

    private final List<LineDetail> matches = new ArrayList<>();

    private static class LineDetail {
        final int lineNumber;
        final String content;

        LineDetail(int lineNumber, String content) {
            this.lineNumber = lineNumber;
            this.content = content;
        }

        @Override
        public String toString() {
            return String.format("Line %d: %s", lineNumber, content);
        }
    }

    /**
     * Counts how many lines in the input match the pattern.
     *
     * @param input      the text to search, may be multiline
     * @param regex      the regular expression
     * @param flags      regex flags, e.g. Pattern.CASE_INSENSITIVE
     * @return           the count of matching lines
     * @throws PatternSyntaxException if the regex is invalid
     */
    public int countLines(String input, String regex, int flags) {
        if (input == null) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Input string cannot be null");
        }

        Pattern pattern;
        try {
            pattern = Pattern.compile(regex, flags);
        } catch (PatternSyntaxException e) {
            throw new PatternSyntaxException(e.getDescription(), e.getRawPattern(), e.getIndex());
        }

        if (input.isEmpty()) {
            return 0;
        }

        String[] lines = input.split("\n", -1);
        matches.clear();

        for (int i = 0; i < lines.length; i++) {
            if (pattern.matcher(lines[i]).find()) {
                matches.add(new LineDetail(i + 1, lines[i]));
            }
        }

        return matches.size();
    }

    /**
     * Returns the matching lines. Call countLines first.
     */
    public List<LineDetail> getMatches() {
        return new ArrayList<>(matches);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LineCounter counter = new LineCounter();

        try (BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in))) {

            System.out.print("Enter the text to search (enter a blank line to finish): ");

            StringBuilder input = new StringBuilder();
            String line;
            while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
                if (line.isEmpty()) {
                    break;
                }
                input.append(line).append("\n");
            }

            System.out.print("Enter the regular expression: ");
            String regex = reader.readLine();
            if (regex == null) {
                regex = "";
            }

            System.out.print("Enter flags (i for case-insensitive, m for multiline, g for global, or none): ");
            String flagInput = reader.readLine();
            int flags = 0;
            if (flagInput != null) {
                if (flagInput.toLowerCase().contains("i")) {
                    flags |= Pattern.CASE_INSENSITIVE;
                }
                if (flagInput.toLowerCase().contains("m")) {
                    flags |= Pattern.MULTILINE;
                }
            }

            try {
                int count = counter.countLines(input.toString(), regex, flags);

                System.out.println("\n--- Results ---");
                System.out.println("Pattern: " + regex);
                System.out.println("Input length: " + input.length() + " characters");
                System.out.println("Lines matching: " + count);

                List<LineDetail> allMatches = counter.getMatches();
                if (!allMatches.isEmpty()) {
                    System.out.println("\nMatching lines:");
                    for (LineDetail m : allMatches) {
                        System.out.println(m);
                    }
                }

            } catch (PatternSyntaxException e) {
                System.err.println("Error: the regular expression is invalid.");
                System.err.println(e.getDescription());
            } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
                System.err.println("Error: " + e.getMessage());
            }

        } catch (IOException e) {
            System.err.println("Error reading input: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

What this does:

It reads text until you hit a blank line, so you can paste a whole file's worth of content. Then it asks for the regex and the flags. The flags are a hint. The program doesn't check that they're real, so type them correctly.

The count comes first, because that's what you asked for. Below it are the matching lines with their line numbers, in order.

Empty input counts as zero matches and isn't an error. An empty regex is valid in Java, and it matches every line, including empty ones. If you'd rather treat an empty regex as a mistake, change the check in `countLines` from `input.isEmpty()` to `input.isEmpty() && regex.isEmpty()`.

The `split("\n", -1)` keeps trailing empty lines. Without the second argument, a string that ends in a newline loses that final line, and you'd count it wrong.

The error handling throws inside `countLines`, and `main` catches it in one place and prints it under "Error:". Each exception carries its own message, so the user hears exactly what went wrong.

