One thing I'd change if it were my program. Right now the flags are a hint, and a typo in the flags goes unnoticed. I'd have `main` check that `flagInput` contains only letters that are flags, and refuse to run, or run without the bad ones and say so. It's your call, though. A program that silently drops a flag can also leave a user believing it ran with one.

The tests are in a separate file, and they cover the empty string, a bad regex, case-insensitive matching and multiline input. I'd write the tests before I'd trust the program. The split logic and the flag parsing are where I'd expect a bug to hide.

I'll write this as a function that does the work, and a small block that runs it when you execute the file. That way the searching can be tested on its own. I'll leave the file open while we go through it, because a search that closes its file halfway through teaches you nothing about searching.

```python
import sys

def grep(filename, query):
    """Yield (line_number, position, line) for every match.

    Line numbers start at 1, as people expect when they read a file.
    Position is the index of the first character of the match in that line.
    The newline at the end of a line is not counted.
    """
    try:
        handle = open(filename, encoding="utf-8-sig")
    except FileNotFoundError:
        raise FileNotFoundError(f"Cannot read {filename!r}: no such file")
    except OSError as e:
        raise OSError(f"Cannot read {filename!r}: {e}")

    try:
        for line_number, line in enumerate(handle, start=1):
            text = line.rstrip("\n")
            position = text.lower().find(query)
            if position != -1:
                yield (line_number, position, line)
    finally:
        handle.close()


if __name__ == "__main__":
    if len(sys.argv) == 1:
        filename = input("Enter the name of the text file: ")
    else:
        filename = sys.argv[1]

    query = input("Enter the string to search for: ")

    if not query:
        print("The search string is empty. Every line matches.")

    try:
        for line_number, position, line in grep(filename, query):
            print(f"{filename}:{line_number}: {line}", end="")
    except (FileNotFoundError, OSError) as e:
        print("Error:", e)
        sys.exit(1)
```

Run it and type the filename when it asks. Or put the filename on the command line, and it will ask only for the string.

The first thing to notice is where the messages go. A message that tells the user their file is missing belongs on the screen, in the open, and it says so. A message that says the search found nothing is a different thing. On a large file a quiet exit is the right behaviour. You can check it with `if not any(grep(...))` without changing a line of this code.

The encoding does more than it seems to. Plain `utf-8` refuses to read a file that ends in a byte nobody understands, and most text files on Windows are not UTF-8. The `-sig` form takes the bytes `ef bb bf` at the very start of the file to mean "this is UTF-8" and reads them as nothing. That is how a file carried from a Windows editor onto a Linux machine stops failing. It also means a file that starts with those three bytes loses them, which is a small surprise, so leave it alone only if you know what you are doing.

The `try` around the loop is where the file gets closed. If an error happens while we are reading, the `finally` still runs, and so does the close. If we read every line and reach the end, the close runs there. If the program crashes, the close is lost, and that is nothing we could have done about it.

The `-i` flag in the Unix tool `grep` does exactly this search. It is worth knowing the tool already exists, because it means most of what this program does has been checked by more people than will check this one.

Three things are left to you. I have assumed the string is to be found as a substring, so searching for "the" finds "other", since "other" holds "the". Set `position = text.lower().find(query)` to `text.lower().find("\\b" + query + "\\b")` and it will match whole words only. I have assumed the file is text. Run it on a spreadsheet and the numbers in the first column will come up wherever those numbers happen to appear inside a line. And I have taken "not found" to mean zero matches. If you would rather hear something at the end when the search comes up empty, add the check I mentioned above, and it will say so.
